From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: promote s_fast_map before writing to it

Writing the first entry of a static array faulted:

    fatal: signal 11 - SIGSEGV (bad address), si_code 2, fault address 0x803f61000
    CPU::CodeCache::AllocateFastMap

⚠ si_code 2 is SEGV_ACCERR - MAPPED BUT NOT PERMITTED - not SEGV_MAPERR. The console's own klog
called the same fault "page not present", which sent the first day of investigation after a
missing-memory bug that does not exist: a probe allocating and touching every page of 1, 8, 32,
64, 105, 128, 160 and 256 MiB through malloc passed with zero mismatches.

The address is the answer. This module loads at 0x800870000 and s_fast_map links at 0x36f1000;
the sum is the fault address exactly. So the memory is this module's OWN .bss, and it is not
writable.

⚠ THIS .prx DECLARES 50 MB OF .bss AGAINST 259 KiB OF FILE DATA - s_code_storage alone is 48 MB -
and the module loader does not give writable pages for all of it. s_fast_map sits roughly 49 MB
in. Whatever the limit is, it is below that and above the .bss that works.

ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c already records this shape for mupen64plus, where a 32 MB static array inside
a module has to be promoted before the recompiler writes to it. That case wants execute; this one
only wants write. s_code_storage needs no line here because it reaches JitCodeBuffer::Initialize,
which mprotects it already.

⚠ AND THIS IS A PLATFORM FINDING, NOT A swanstation ONE. Any core with a large .bss will meet it,
and it will look like a different bug each time.

diff --git a/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp b/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp
--- a/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp
+++ b/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp
@@ -1,4 +1,8 @@
 #include "cpu_code_cache.h"
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+#include <cerrno>
+#include <sys/mman.h>
+#endif
 #include "bus.h"
 #include "common/log.h"
 #include "cpu_core.h"
@@ -100,6 +104,38 @@
 
 static void AllocateFastMap()
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+  // ⚠ THE MODULE'S OWN .bss IS NOT ALL WRITABLE ON THIS CONSOLE, AND THAT IS WHAT KILLED THIS
+  // FUNCTION. Measured: writing s_fast_map[0] faulted with
+  //
+  //     fatal: signal 11 - SIGSEGV, si_code 2, fault address 0x803f61000
+  //
+  // si_code 2 is SEGV_ACCERR - mapped, but not permitted - and 0x803f61000 is exactly this
+  // module's load base plus s_fast_map's link address. This .prx declares 50 MB of .bss against
+  // 259 KiB of file data (s_code_storage alone is 48 MB), and the module loader does not appear
+  // to hand out writable pages for all of it; s_fast_map sits about 49 MB in.
+  //
+  // The same shape is already recorded for mupen64plus in ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c: a large static
+  // array inside a module needs its pages PROMOTED before use. That one wants execute; this one
+  // only wants write. s_code_storage does not need a line here because it reaches
+  // JitCodeBuffer::Initialize, which mprotects it already.
+  //
+  // ⚠ ROUNDED OUTWARDS. sceKernelMprotect works in 16 KiB granules - the platform's real page
+  // size, not the 4096 most code assumes - so rounding the base up would leave the first pages
+  // unprotected, which are precisely the ones written first.
+  {
+    const uintptr_t granule = 16u * 1024u;
+    uintptr_t       base    = reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(s_fast_map) & ~(granule - 1);
+    uintptr_t       end     = (reinterpret_cast<uintptr_t>(s_fast_map) + sizeof(s_fast_map)
+                               + granule - 1) & ~(granule - 1);
+    if (mprotect(reinterpret_cast<void*>(base), end - base, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE) != 0)
+      Log_ErrorPrintf("orbis: mprotect(s_fast_map, %zu KiB) failed, errno %d - the write below "
+                      "will fault", (size_t)((end - base) / 1024), errno);
+    else
+      Log_InfoPrintf("orbis: s_fast_map promoted to read-write, %zu KiB at %p",
+                     (size_t)((end - base) / 1024), reinterpret_cast<void*>(base));
+  }
+#endif
   static constexpr VirtualMemoryAddress ranges[][2] = {
     {0x00000000, 0x00800000}, // RAM
     {0x1F000000, 0x1F800000}, // EXP1
